YOUR TASK: Complete this task: Write a complete standalone Python program (output only code) that solves the problem using the bad-pair theorem. Use 1-based person indices for `ans`. For each person set `l = min(S,T)-1`, `r = max(S,T)-1`, and `sign = 0` if `S < T` else `1`. First fill `ans[j]` for same-`l` or same-`r` bad pairs using `last_l`/`last_r` in O(M). Then compute same-sign strict-crossing bad pairs with CDQ over person index `0..M-1`, using `BASE = 128`. Maintain and pass down lists of indices sorted by `l` and by `r`; split them by `mid`. Base case: brute force all pairs in the block, scanning `i` backwards and breaking on the first same-sign strict crossing. Cross case: use two sweeps. Type A (`l_i < l_j < r_i < r_j`): sweep right half by increasing `r`, add left intervals with `r_i < r_j`, and for each added `i` do a range-max update on the compressed sorted list of right-half `l`-values over `(l_i, r_i)` with value `i+1` and sign `s_i`; query point `l_j` in the sign-specific tree and update `ans[j+1]`. Type B (`l_j < l_i < r_j < r_i`): sweep right half by decreasing `l`, add left intervals with `l_i > l_j`, do range-max updates on the compressed sorted list of right-half `r`-values over `(l_i, r_i)`, and query point `r_j`. Implement the range-max update / point-query segment tree with timestamps: global arrays `mark`, `val0`, `val1` sized `2 * power_of_two(M)`; each sweep gets a new stamp and a local `sz = power_of_two(number of right points)`; range `[a,b)` is found by `bisect_right(coords, l_i)` and `bisect_left(coords, r_i)`; point position by `bisect_left(coords, coord)`; when a node is first touched in the current stamp set both vals to 0 then set the sign's val to `v`, otherwise max. After CDQ build prefix max `pref[i] = max(pref[i-1], ans[i])` and answer query `L R` with `Yes` iff `pref[R] < L`. Ensure it passes both samples; do not add unrelated optimizations.